\subsection{GRPO Responsiveness and Scale}
\label{sec:decoupling}

\input{tables/tab2_decoupling}

Table~\ref{tab:decoupling} shows that GRPO accuracy changes are small across all pruned methods ($|\Delta| < 1$\,pp: CKA-only $+$0.48, Standard $+$0.55, \rasp{} $-$0.61\,pp) despite the 4.4$\times$ peak reward gap between CKA-only and Standard.
The unpruned model maintains 81.27\% while reward rises to 0.669, confirming reward-accuracy decoupling under GSM8K's binary reward.
This decoupling holds even at 25\% sparsity, where Standard's peak reward collapses to 0.008 yet accuracy changes by only $+$0.99\,pp (single seed; Appendix~\ref{sec:appendix_cross_sparsity}).
Extended training to 300 steps yields unchanged accuracy (CKA-only: 64.37\%$\to$64.37\%, Standard: 59.44\%$\to$59.29\%; single seed), confirming that reward gains do not translate to accuracy improvements within our training horizon.
This decoupling extends across MATH-500~\citep{hendrycks2021math}, HumanEval~\citep{chen2021humaneval}, and MBPP~\citep{austin2021mbpp} (all GRPO deltas $< 2$\,pp; Appendices~\ref{sec:appendix_math500},~\ref{sec:appendix_mbpp}), while the pruning-criterion ranking transfers to MATH-500 (CKA-only 29.0\% vs Standard 26.4\% vs \rasp{} 22.6\%).
\label{sec:ood}
ShortGPT~\citep{shortgpt2024} depth pruning shows task-dependent GRPO interaction ($-$1.36\,pp on GSM8K, $+$4.01\,pp on MBPP; Tables~\ref{tab:decoupling},~\ref{tab:mbpp}), unlike the uniformly neutral deltas of fine-grained methods ($|\Delta| \leq 1.34$\,pp).

