    \item We show that \textbf{reward-accuracy decoupling} is sparsity-dependent: at 15\% sparsity, GRPO amplifies the CKA-only advantage ($+$1.51\,pp differential), while at 20--25\% GRPO cannot improve upon SFT baselines. This motivates reward trajectory as the diagnostic for recovery capacity (\S\ref{sec:decoupling}).
    \item We identify \textbf{preserved representational diversity} as a plausible mechanism: CKA-only ($\mu{=}0.62$ diagonal CKA) diverges most from the dense model, while collapsing methods preserve dense fidelity ($\mu{\geq}0.88$). Cross-architecture (Qwen2.5-Math-7B) and cross-scale (1.5B) experiments provide initial evidence for generality (\S\ref{sec:extended_analysis}).
